                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Laurence C. Vass of the Office of
                        European Regional Affairs</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >confidential</hi></seg></opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fiscal Year</hi> 55 <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Aid Program</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">Basic Assumptions</hi></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>NATO build up nearly over. Must emphasize qualitative improvement while
                        work on problem of reducing requirements.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>53 AR will result in few additional units in 55 goals, except for Germany
                        (if EDC ratification probable before July).</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>This creates over-funding problem, since will start FY 55 with at least $8
                        billion in pipeline.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Europe will have no $ balance of payments problem in FY 55, as result of
                        extraordinary military expenditures in FY 54 of over $1 billion, and about
                        $1.5 billion in FY 55.</item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>IC must be treated as part of “hot war” in Far East. France constitutes
                        only serious budget supporting problem likely in FY 55.</item>
                </list>
